// A theme style has no bare name: reading it that way is refused, and the
// refusal names the owning theme. That refusal has one exception -- the only
// one -- when an ordinary GLOBAL already carries that name: the bare name then
// denotes that global, as it always has, and the refusal falls silent.
//
// EN mirror of the FR fixture theme_style_globale_homonyme (invariant 3,
// bilingual). The two core facts of  are locked in EN canonical forms:
//   1. the bare name yields the value of the ordinary global;
//   2. it does NOT yield the theme style value of the same name;
//   3. the style stays reachable through its qualified form.

Trace("=== theme style homonym of an ordinary global (EN) ===")

// --- 1. The bare name yields the ORDINARY global ---
dbgCheckEqual(stAmbiance..InputZone..BackgroundColor, RGB(250, 250, 250),
"bare homonym name: yields the ordinary global value")

// --- 2. It does not yield the theme style (no leak) ---
dbgCheckDifferent(stAmbiance..InputZone..BackgroundColor, RGB(200, 10, 10),
"bare homonym name: the theme style value does not leak")

// --- 3. The theme style stays readable through its qualified form ---
dbgCheckEqual(theme.stAmbiance..InputZone..BackgroundColor, RGB(200, 10, 10),
"qualified form: yields the theme style value, despite the homonym")
dbgCheckDifferent(theme.stAmbiance..InputZone..BackgroundColor, RGB(250, 250, 250),
"qualified form: the ordinary global does not mask the style")

// --- 4. Counter-check: without a homonym, the bare name stays closed ---
// `stNoHomonym` has no ordinary global to carry it: its bare name is refused
// at COMPILE time, so there is nothing to read here. What we verify is that
// the exception above did not open the general case.
dbgCheckEqual(theme.stNoHomonym..InputZone..BackgroundColor, RGB(20, 180, 20),
"style without homonym: readable via the theme namespace")

dbgTestSummary()
